    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The flute is an aerophone — sound from air splitting across an edge or hole. Concert flute (Boehm system, 19th century) is metal with complex keywork. Tin whistle (penny whistle) is a simple six-hole fipple flute central to Irish trad."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "concert-flute",
      "title": "Concert flute",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "C instrument, range roughly C4–C7. Silver, gold, or nickel alloy. Embouchure (lip shape) controls tone. Baroque flute was wooden, one key."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tin-whistle",
      "title": "Tin whistle",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Typically D major (most common session key) but available in many keys. Diatonic with cross-fingerings for accidentals. Clarke, Generation, and Feadóg are classic brands. Simple enough for beginners; expressive in expert hands (Mary Bergin, Joanie Madden)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "wooden-flute",
      "title": "Wooden flute",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Irish traditional wooden flute (concert pitch) — breathy, reedy tone. Keyless or keyed. 8-key and 4-key systems common."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
